course: everyday
lesson: 268
title: In the car: seat belts, car seats and good passengers
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A seat belt holds your body to the seat in a crash, but only when it sits in the right place: the lap belt low across the hips and the shoulder belt across the chest and shoulder, not the neck.
explain: Adult belts are made for adult bodies, so a child uses a booster seat until the belt fits, usually until about 145 to 150 cm (4 feet 9 inches) tall, and sits in the back until about 12 or 13.
explain: Front airbags open with great force; they protect adults but can badly hurt children, and a baby in a rear-facing seat must never sit in front of an active airbag.
explain: A good passenger keeps arms, head and fingers inside, leaves the keys and controls alone, talks quietly so the driver can concentrate, and gets out on the pavement side.
explain: A girl in Colorado opens her car door with her far hand; her body turns, she looks back and sees a cyclist coming, so she waits — this is called the Dutch reach.
fact: The Dutch reach means opening the car door with the hand farther from it, which turns your body so you look back for bikes.
fact: At school drop-off you get out on the pavement side quickly and walk away from the cars, because drivers there are in a hurry.
fact: Children leave car keys, locks and controls alone, because a child can start, move or lock the car by accident.
fact: Arms and heads stay inside the car, because passing cars, posts and branches can hit you.
fact: Fingers stay clear of a closing power window, because it can trap and hurt them.
fact: Passengers talk quietly and settle arguments calmly, because the driver must concentrate on the road.
fact: Carsickness gets better by looking out of the front window at the horizon and getting fresh air; reading or looking at a screen makes it worse.
fact: Using a phone while driving causes many crashes and is against the law in many places, and a child may kindly remind a parent.
fact: The lap belt goes low across the hips and the shoulder belt across the chest and shoulder, not across the neck, and never behind your back or under your arm.
fact: A child uses a booster seat until the adult belt fits, usually around 145 to 150 cm or 4 feet 9 inches tall.
fact: Children under about 12 or 13 sit in the back seat where possible, because front airbags open with great force.
fact: A rear-facing baby seat is never put in front of an active airbag.
fact: The seat belt stays on until the car has stopped and the engine is off.
fact: Everyone needs their own seat belt; two children never share one and nobody rides on a lap.
fact: In a crash the pull on a body is far stronger than any arms, so nobody can hold a child or baby safely on their lap.
fact: Many crashes happen on short trips close to home, so you buckle up for every trip, however short.
exercise: What is the Dutch reach? | Opening the door with the far hand, so you turn and look back | Reaching for the seat belt with both hands | Opening the door as quickly as you can | Leaning out of the window to check the road
exercise: Why is the Dutch reach a good habit? | It makes you turn and see bikes coming from behind | It opens the door wider and faster than the normal way | It stops the door making a noise | It is the only way the handle works
exercise: Your parent drops you at school. How do you get out? | Quickly on the pavement side, then walk away from the cars | On the road side, because it is nearer the gate | Slowly, chatting with the driver first while the cars queue | Through the boot at the back
exercise: Your little sister wants to play with the car keys. What do you tell her? | Leave keys alone; she could start or lock the car | Keys are fine to play with when the car is parked and the engine is off | She can press any button except the horn | Only the radio button is dangerous
exercise: Why must you keep your arms and head inside the car? | Passing cars, posts and branches can hit you | The wind will make your arm cold and numb very quickly | The driver cannot see the mirrors properly then | It is rude to wave at other drivers on the road
exercise: Your brother closes the power window. What do you do with your fingers? | Keep them clear, because the window can trap them | Put them on the glass to slow it down | Hold the top of the glass to stop it | Push the button again to stop it with your hand there
exercise: You and your sister start arguing loudly in the car. What should you do? | Talk quietly and settle it so the driver can concentrate | Shout louder so the driver can sort it out | Kick the seat to show you are angry | Unbuckle and move to another seat further away from your sister
exercise: You start to feel carsick. What helps? | Looking out of the front window at the horizon | Reading your book closer to your face | Playing a game on a tablet | Closing every window to keep warm
exercise: Your dad reads a text while driving. What can you do? | Kindly remind him that phones are dangerous while driving | Grab the phone out of his hand | Say nothing, because adults know best | Read the messages out loud for him so he can keep his eyes on the road
exercise: Where should the shoulder part of a seat belt go? | Across the chest and shoulder | Across the neck, close to the chin | Behind your back, out of the way | Under your arm, where it is comfier
exercise: Where should the lap part of a seat belt go? | Low across the hips | High across the tummy | Around the knees, below the hips | Across the chest, under the arms
exercise: A 9-year-old who is 130 cm tall gets in the car. What should she sit on? | A booster seat, as the adult belt does not fit her yet | The front seat with the airbag | The adult seat with no booster, since she is over nine years old | Her older brother's lap
exercise: Why do children under about 12 or 13 sit in the back seat? | Front airbags open with great force and can hurt them | The back seat is warmer in winter | Children are not allowed to see the road ahead through the front window | Only adults may talk to the driver
exercise: Where must a baby in a rear-facing car seat never go? | In front of an active airbag | In the back seat, on either side | Behind the driver in the back row | In the middle of the back seat
exercise: The car is pulling into your driveway. When do you take off your belt? | When the car has stopped and the engine is off | As soon as you see your house | When the car slows down at the corner | When you hear the indicator clicking
exercise: There are three children and only two seat belts. What is the answer? | Nobody shares a belt or rides on a lap; make another trip | Two children share one belt | The smallest sits on a lap | The smallest sits on the floor between the seats, where nobody can see her
exercise: Why can a parent not hold a child safely in a crash? | Crash forces are far stronger than any arms | Parents always hold their children tightly enough | A child is too light to move at all in a crash | Arms work better than seat belts for small children
test: A boy in Leeds tucks the shoulder belt behind his back because it rubs his neck. What is the right fix? | Use a booster seat so the belt sits across his shoulder | Keep it behind his back, since the lap belt alone is enough | Put it under his arm instead | Take the belt off for short trips
test: A cyclist rides past just as a passenger flings the car door open. Which habit would have helped? | The Dutch reach, which turns you to look back | Opening the door faster | Honking the horn before opening | Opening the door with the near hand, then checking the mirror
test: Which child should sit in the front seat? | Neither, if there is room in the back | A 10-year-old, because she is big enough | A 7-year-old, so a parent can watch him | Any child who asks the driver politely first
test: A mum in Alberta says, "We're nearly home, you can unbuckle now." Why is it better to wait? | A crash or hard brake can still happen at the last moment | The belt is hard to undo while moving | It is impolite to unbuckle early | The car will beep loudly and stop the engine if anyone unbuckles
test: A girl in Sydney reads a comic on a winding road and feels sick. What would help most? | Putting the comic away and looking out at the horizon | Reading faster to finish before she feels worse | Holding the comic closer | Sitting with her eyes on her lap
test: A boy waves his arm out of the window on a country road. What could happen? | A branch, post or passing car could hit his arm | The wind could blow his watch off only | Nothing, because country roads are empty | Drivers behind him will slow down because they think he is signalling
test: Why do adult seat belts not fit most 9-year-olds? | They are made for adults and sit on the neck and tummy | They are too short to reach a child | They only work in the front seat | Children are too heavy for them and would break them in a sudden stop
test: An uncle says he will hold the baby on his lap on a short drive. Which is true? | No arms can hold a baby in a crash; it needs a car seat | A short drive is safe without a car seat | Holding the baby is safer than a car seat | The baby is safe if the uncle wears his own belt tightly around them both
test: Two children start a pillow fight in the back seat. Why is it dangerous? | It distracts the driver, who must watch the road | Pillows can make the car go faster | It wears out the seats | The airbags open when someone shouts in the car or bangs a pillow on the seat
test: belt's rubbing my neck, gonna put it behind my back | No, keep it on, ask for a booster so it fits | Yes, behind your back is fine | Tuck it under your arm | Take it off till you get there
test: just 5 mins to nan's, can me n jess share one belt? | No, everyone needs their own belt | Yes, for short trips | Yes, if you sit very close together and hold on tight | Sit on mum's lap instead
test: feel carsick, gonna play on my tablet to forget it | Look out the front at the horizon | Read a book instead | Close your eyes and play games | Look down at your feet till it passes
test: dad's texting while driving 🙄 | Kindly remind him it's dangerous | Ask him to text faster | Read the messages to him loudly | Grab the phone off him
explain: A boy in Alabama took off his seat belt for a short drive to the shop; when the car braked hard he was thrown into the seat in front and broke his nose and two teeth.
explain: A girl of eight in Canada rode in the front seat; in a small crash the airbag burst out with great force and injured her neck.
fact: Seat belts and the right car seat or booster stop many children being killed or badly hurt in crashes every year.
fact: An airbag opens with enough force to injure a small child, which is why children ride in the back seat.
test: just 2 mins to the shop, no belt needed | Always buckle up, most crashes happen near home | Fine, short trips are safe | Fine if you hold on to the seat in front | Only on the motorway, where the cars go fast
test: Why do children under about 12 or 13 ride in the back seat? | A front airbag can injure a small child | The back seat is warmer | It is only to stop them fighting over the radio | The front seat is too high
test: lil bro unbuckled himself in the back | Tell the driver so they can stop safely and buckle him | Ignore it, he's only small | Unbuckle yourself to help him | Hold him on your lap for the rest of the trip, that's just as safe
test: did u hear a kid sat in the front seat, the airbag went off in a small bump and broke his nose? real lesson? | Children ride in the back; airbags open with great force | Airbags are soft like pillows | Sit closer to the airbag so it's gentler | Airbags only open in big crashes on motorways, never in small bumps
test: apparently 2 kids shared one seat belt on the way to footy and when the car braked they both smashed into the seat. what's the true lesson? | Everyone needs their own seat belt | Two small kids can share one belt if they're small enough | Hold on tight instead of a belt | Short trips don't need belts
test: my mate says putting the shoulder belt behind ur back is fine cos it's comfier. right? | No, it goes across the chest and shoulder | Yes, comfort matters most | Yes, the lap belt is enough on its own | Yes, on short trips
exam: Identify the correct position of the lap belt. | Low across the hips | Across the stomach, above the belly button | Across the neck | Behind the back
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of a booster seat? | It is used until the adult belt fits, about 145 to 150 cm | It is used only by babies | It is used only on long journeys of more than one hour | It is required only in the front seat
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice regarding a rear-facing baby seat. | It is never placed in front of an active airbag | It is placed in the front for the driver to see | It is placed facing forwards after a month | It may be held on a lap instead
exam: Ascertain the technique termed the «Dutch reach». | Opening the door with the far hand, to look back for bikes | Reaching across to fasten another's seat belt | Leaning out of the window to wave | Opening the door quickly with the nearer hand, then glancing in the mirror
